# Terraform
.terraform/
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfvars
*.tfvars.secret

# Saved plan files. A saved plan embeds a full state snapshot with every data
# source resolved, in plaintext. environments/shadow/tfplan was committed this
# way and leaked the AWS account ID and a named IAM principal (finding W8-1).
tfplan
*.tfplan

# Lock files are TRACKED, everywhere. (PF-622, PRD p.5 "no unpinned versions
# permitted".)
#
# This rule used to be the inverse -- a blanket `.terraform.lock.hcl` ignore with
# two negations for local-config and render -- and its own comment already
# argued against itself: "New configs commit theirs, a committed lock file is
# what makes `terraform apply` reproducible on a clean machine." That is true of
# the graded root too, and the graded root was on the ignored side of the line.
#
# Why it matters more than it looks. An exact pin in `required_providers` fixes
# the version; the lock file fixes the *checksums*. Without it a CI runner can
# resolve 5.100.0 from a mirror serving a different artifact and nothing in the
# configuration notices. It is also what makes `terraform init -lockfile=readonly`
# meaningful: with no committed lock file there is nothing to hold readonly and
# the flag silently succeeds against whatever was just downloaded.
#
# Every root now carries one generated for BOTH darwin_arm64 (the operator
# workstation) and linux_amd64 (CI). A lock file with only the author's platform
# hashes fails on the runner with a checksum error that reads like a supply-chain
# attack and is in fact just a missing platform.
!.terraform.lock.hcl

# Crash logs
crash.log
crash.*.log

# Override files
override.tf
override.tf.json
*_override.tf
*_override.tf.json

# Sensitive variable files
*.auto.tfvars.secret
